When a user calls `V3Vault::liquidate` to liquidate a position, the vault will attempt to transfer the position's NFT to the owner using `safeTransferFrom`.

Notice `_cleanupLoan` call inside `V3Vault::liquidate` function:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/main/src/V3Vault.sol#L744

```solidity
        _cleanupLoan(params.tokenId, state.newDebtExchangeRateX96, state.newLendExchangeRateX96, owner);
```

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-03-revert-lend/blob/main/src/V3Vault.sol#L1077-L1085

```solidity

    function _cleanupLoan(uint256 tokenId, uint256 debtExchangeRateX96, uint256 lendExchangeRateX96, address owner)
        internal
    {
        _removeTokenFromOwner(owner, tokenId);
        _updateAndCheckCollateral(tokenId, debtExchangeRateX96, lendExchangeRateX96, loans[tokenId].debtShares, 0);
        delete loans[tokenId];
@>        nonfungiblePositionManager.safeTransferFrom(address(this), owner, tokenId);
        emit Remove(tokenId, owner);
    }

```

`nonfungiblePositionManager` contract calls `onERC721Received` on the recipient after `safeTransferFrom`. 

A malicious user can avoid liquidation by creating a collateralized position calling `V3Vault::create`, passing a smart contract address as the `recipient` parameter and having the smart contract revert `onERC721Received`. The malicious user can set the contract to reject or accept the NFT transfer to his convenience.

As a consequence, undercollateralized positions cannot be liquidated, leading to protocol debts that can't be reduced.

